    "primary_indication": "Small cell lung cancer",
    "indications": "Doxorubicin is an anthracycline to poisomerase II inhibitor indicated for: • Ovarian cancer: After failure of platinum-based chemotherapy. • AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma: After failure of prior systemic chemotherapy or intolerance to such therapy. Doxorubicin is an anthracycline to poisomerase II inhibitor indicated for: • Ovarian cancer: After failure of platinum-based chemotherapy. • AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma: After failure of prior systemic chemotherapy or intolerance to such therapy. • Multiple Myeloma: In combination with bortezomib in patients who have not previously received bortezomib and have received at least one prior therapy.",
    "how_it_works_bn": "Doxorubicin is a cytotoxic anthracycline antibiotic. The cytotoxic action results from its binding to DNA and inhibition of nucleic acid synthesis. Doxorubicin has been shown to produce regression in a variety of disseminated malignancies.",
    "dosage_bn": "Administer Doxorubicin at an initial rate of 1 mg/min to minimize the risk of infusion reactions. If no infusion related reactions occur, increase rate of infusion to complete administration over 1 hour. Do not administer as bolus injection or undiluted solution. • Ovarian cancer: 50 mg/m2 IV every 4 weeks • AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma: 20 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks • Multiple Myeloma: 30 mg/m2 IV on day 4 following bortezomib",
    "side_effects_bn": "Leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea. Rarely facial flushing, rash, alopecia. Blurred vision, headache, seizures, paraesthesia, confusion, malaise, lethargy, skin pigmentation.",
